<?phem

namespace App\Ui\Demos;

use Phem\Attribute\Page;
use Phem\Node;

use function App\Ui\{DemoPage, Example, Examples, Hero};
use function Phem\Ui\Base\Examples\{PreviewCardAnimatedDemo, PreviewCardControlledDemo, PreviewCardDetachedDemo, PreviewCardHeroDemo, PreviewCardMultipleDemo};

#[Page('/components/preview-card')]
function PreviewCardPage(): Node
{
    return (
        <DemoPage slug="preview-card">
            <Hero source="PreviewCardHeroDemo">
                <PreviewCardHeroDemo />
            </Hero>
            <Examples>
                <Example title="Detached triggers" source="PreviewCardDetachedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A preview card can be controlled by a trigger located either inside or outside the <code>PreviewCard</code> component. For simple, one-off interactions, place the <code>PreviewCardTrigger</code> inside <code>PreviewCard</code>, as shown in the example at the top of this page.</p>
                        <p>However, if defining the preview card's content next to its trigger is not practical, you can use a detached trigger. This involves placing the <code>PreviewCardTrigger</code> outside of <code>PreviewCard</code> and linking them with the same <code>handle</code>. The root gets the handle as its <code>id</code>, and the link gets <code>data-phem-popover-for</code>.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <PreviewCardDetachedDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Multiple triggers" source="PreviewCardMultipleDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A single preview card can be opened by multiple trigger elements. You can achieve this by using the same <code>handle</code> for several detached triggers, or by placing multiple <code>PreviewCardTrigger</code> components inside a single <code>PreviewCard</code>.</p>
                        <p>The preview card can render different content depending on which trigger opened it. Give each trigger a <code>payload</code>, and put a <code>PreviewCardPayload</code> with the same <code>name</code> in the <code>PreviewCardViewport</code>: the viewport shows a copy of the content of the active trigger.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <PreviewCardMultipleDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Controlled mode with multiple triggers" source="PreviewCardControlledDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>You can control the preview card's open state with the <code>defaultOpen</code> and <code>triggerId</code> props of <code>PreviewCard</code>: a render with new values opens the card next to the link with that <code>id</code>. The controller keeps the values of the browser in <code>data-phem-popover-open-value</code> and <code>data-phem-popover-trigger-id-value</code>.</p>
                        <p>Here, the button is in the root, and opens the card without a request with the <code>phem-popover#open</code> action and a <code>triggerId</code> param. The focus stays on the button.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <PreviewCardControlledDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Animating the Preview Card" source="PreviewCardAnimatedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>You can animate a preview card as it moves between different trigger elements. This includes animating its position, size, and content.</p>
                        <p>To animate the preview card's position, apply CSS transitions to the <code>top</code> and <code>left</code> properties of the <code>PreviewCardPopup</code>. To animate its size, transition its <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> to <code>var(--popup-width)</code> and <code>var(--popup-height)</code>.</p>
                        <p>To enable content animations, wrap the content in the <code>PreviewCardViewport</code> part. It gets a <code>data-activation-direction</code> attribute that indicates the new trigger's position relative to the previous one: up to two tokens, <code>left</code> or <code>right</code> and <code>up</code> or <code>down</code>. Match a single token with the <code>~=</code> attribute selector. Inside the viewport, the content is wrapped in <code>div</code>s with <code>data-current</code> (the incoming content) and <code>data-previous</code> (the outgoing content).</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <PreviewCardAnimatedDemo />
                </Example>
            </Examples>
        </DemoPage>
    );
}
